From: OpenWrt zynq/s9
Subject: mtd: rawnand: pl35x: advertise subpage read/write for on-die ECC
 chips

Chips using NAND_ECC_ENGINE_TYPE_ON_DIE are capable of subpage reads,
and generally of subpage writes too, but the driver never set the
NAND core capability flags for it, so the core always fell back to
full-page accesses. Set NAND_SUBPAGE_READ and clear
NAND_NO_SUBPAGE_WRITE for on-die ECC chips, matching how the core
already lets per-controller drivers opt in to subpage access.

This depends on the paired nand_micron.c patch that actually
implements chip->ecc.read_subpage/write_subpage for Micron on-die ECC
parts; without it the new capability flags are set but unused.

--- a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/pl35x-nand-controller.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/pl35x-nand-controller.c
@@ -938,6 +938,15 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+static void pl35x_nand_setup_ondie_ecc(struct nand_chip *chip)
+{
+	/* NAND with on-die ECC supports subpage reads */
+	chip->options |= NAND_SUBPAGE_READ;
+
+	/* NAND with on-die ECC may support subpage writes */
+	chip->options &= ~(NAND_NO_SUBPAGE_WRITE);
+}
+
 static int pl35x_nand_attach_chip(struct nand_chip *chip)
 {
 	const struct nand_ecc_props *requirements =
@@ -972,6 +981,7 @@
 
 	switch (chip->ecc.engine_type) {
 	case NAND_ECC_ENGINE_TYPE_ON_DIE:
+		pl35x_nand_setup_ondie_ecc(chip);
 		/* Keep these legacy BBT descriptors for ON_DIE situations */
 		chip->bbt_td = &bbt_main_descr;
 		chip->bbt_md = &bbt_mirror_descr;
